---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AlkhDivider from "../components/AlkhDivider.astro";
import Footnote from "../components/Footnote.astro";
import Seal from "../components/Seal.astro";
import VerticalName from "../components/VerticalName.astro";
import { person, pullquote } from "../data/site";
import { url } from "../lib/url";

// Footnotes in the order they appear on this page; they're numbered 1, 2, 3… from this.
Astro.locals.notes = ["name", "seal", "alkh", "knot"];
---

<Base
    title={`My story · ${person.name}`}
    description="Where the work comes from: northern Mongolia, mathematics, many classrooms, and the people who made each next step possible."
>
    <article class="story container" id="top">
        <a class="back" href={url("")}><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back to the main page</a>

        <header class="story-head">
            <VerticalName />
            <div>
                <p class="label">My story</p>
                <h1>Where the work comes from.</h1>
                <p class="lede">
                    Northern Mongolia, mathematics, many classrooms, and the people who made each
                    next step possible.
                </p>
            </div>
        </header>

        <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="background">
            <h2 id="background"><span class="label">01</span> Background</h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    I grew up in Mörön, in the northern part of Mongolia, a full day’s drive from
                    Ulaanbaatar, the world’s coldest capital. Life there wasn’t extraordinary; people
                    mostly minded their own business, and my parents were no different. They had
                    never left our town or gone to college, but they gave my sister and me a happy
                    childhood. At first this seemed like a limitation. But, I now see it as my greatest
                    advantage: because they had never experienced college or life abroad, they never
                    hesitated to let me chase every opportunity I could find.
                </p>
                <p>
                    That freedom let me study math for hours without interruption, even though I was
                    the strongest at home and could have helped with the physically demanding chores.
                    Eventually it opened doors I could never have imagined. After winning a silver
                    medal at the national math olympiad, I received a full scholarship to one of
                    Mongolia’s most prestigious high schools and moved to the capital alone. This was
                    during Covid, and living by myself at such a young age was the hardest challenge
                    I had faced. But it also gave me focus. I chased my high school dream: to be
                    selected as one of the six top math students in the country, represent Mongolia,
                    and win a medal at the International Mathematical Olympiad. I did. I had
                    fulfilled my dream, and I was ready for the next chapter.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Growing up in a rural town, I never realized how much English mattered. No one
                    around me spoke it, except my older sister in later years. Luckily, the principal
                    of my high school noticed my determination and encouraged me to apply to United
                    World College in Maastricht, the Netherlands. For two years I lived among students
                    from around the world, learning English, embracing new cultures, and discovering
                    who I could become. Those years changed me the most. I went from a boy leaving
                    his country for the first time to someone confident facing any challenge, with
                    friends across six continents.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Now I’m in the U.S., studying at a liberal arts college. I’m exploring a wide
                    range of subjects, from art history and environmental studies to economics, and
                    discovering new passions in computer science and data science. I’m meeting
                    people from different backgrounds, learning from them, and shaping the next
                    chapter of my life. I’m not here because I was born into wealth or privilege, but
                    because of supportive parents, inspiring teachers, and friends I could always
                    count on.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="quote">
                <div class="signed">
                    <blockquote>{pullquote}</blockquote>
                    <div class="sign" data-note-anchor>
                        <Seal />
                        <Footnote id="seal" />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>
    </article>

    <AlkhDivider note />

    <article class="story container">
        <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="interests">
            <h2 id="interests"><span class="label">02</span> Interests</h2>
            <div class="cards">
                <div class="card">
                    <p class="label">Basketball</p>
                    <blockquote class="small">
                        “Three seconds left, Batsambuu catches the ball, three, two, one, he shoots
                        it, BANGGG! BUZZER BEATER!”
                    </blockquote>
                    <p>
                        That’s how seven-year-old me commentated on myself, playing basketball with
                        socks for a ball and a chair for a basket, after watching the 2010 NBA
                        Finals. One player, Kobe, stood out from the rest, and seeing the joy he
                        brought to twenty thousand fans at Staples Center showed me what a great
                        sport basketball is. Since then I’ve enjoyed everything about it: playing,
                        watching, the stories around it, and even the video games.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="card">
                    <p class="label">Video games</p>
                    <blockquote class="small">
                        “Wanting something does not give you the right to have it.”
                        <cite>Assassin’s Creed II</cite>
                    </blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Video games are how I relax and step away from reality. I often prefer a good
                        game to a movie or a book, because games combine art and technology so
                        beautifully. They teach lessons, stir a wide range of emotions, and let you
                        do things you never would in real life: arguing with friends over a choice,
                        losing myself in a puzzle, or riding a horse across the open world of Ancient
                        Egypt. It’s a kind of storytelling that makes you part of the story.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="beliefs">
            <h2 id="beliefs"><span class="label">03</span> On education</h2>
            <div class="prose">
                <p>
                    I believe better education is the key to a better world. I have studied in a
                    rural public school, a prestigious private school, an international boarding
                    school, and now a liberal arts college, and each taught me something different
                    about the power of good teaching. Because of that, I believe education should be
                    accessible to everyone, no matter where they are born.
                </p>
                <p>
                    It has become a central part of my dream to build a world-class educational
                    institution, giving more children the chance to discover and pursue their
                    passions. As steps toward it, I tutor high school students in Mongolia as much as
                    I can, run free olympiad camps and online contests, and built{" "}
                    <a href="https://khu-math.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KHU Math</a>{" "}
                    so that a student in any town can practice the way I did.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <a class="back back-end" href={url("")}><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back to the main page</a>
    </article>
</Base>

<style>
    .story {
        max-width: 860px;
    }
    .back {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 8px;
        min-height: 44px;
        margin-top: 20px;
        font-size: 0.9rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        text-decoration: none;
        color: var(--ink-2);
    }
    .back-end {
        margin-block: 8px clamp(36px, 6vw, 64px);
    }
    .story-head {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
        column-gap: clamp(16px, 3vw, 32px);
        align-items: stretch;
        padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px) clamp(20px, 3vw, 36px);
    }
    .story-head .label {
        margin-bottom: 14px;
    }
    h1 {
        font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 1.6rem + 5vw, 5rem);
        line-height: 0.98;
        letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    }
    .lede {
        margin-top: 18px;
        max-width: 44ch;
        font-family: var(--serif);
        font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 1rem + 0.8vw, 1.5rem);
        color: var(--ink-2);
    }
    .chapter {
        padding-block: clamp(28px, 4.5vw, 48px);
    }
    .chapter h2 {
        display: flex;
        align-items: baseline;
        gap: 14px;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 1.4rem + 1.6vw, 2.5rem);
    }
    .chapter h2 .label {
        color: var(--accent);
    }
    .prose {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1.1em;
        max-width: 64ch;
        font-size: 1.06rem;
        line-height: 1.75;
        color: var(--ink-2);
    }
    /* drop cap spanning exactly two lines: top aligned with the first line's capitals,
       bottom on the second line's baseline */
    .prose p:first-child::first-letter {
        -webkit-initial-letter: 2;
        initial-letter: 2;
        margin-right: 0.14em;
        font-family: var(--serif);
        color: var(--ink);
    }
    @supports not ((initial-letter: 2) or (-webkit-initial-letter: 2)) {
        .prose p:first-child::first-letter {
            float: left;
            margin: 0.1em 0.12em 0 0;
            font-size: 3.5em;
            line-height: 0.82;
        }
    }
    .quote {
        margin-top: clamp(32px, 5vw, 52px);
        padding-top: clamp(24px, 3.5vw, 32px);
        border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
    }
    /* the seal signs the quote at its lower right */
    .signed {
        display: grid;
        width: fit-content;
        max-width: 100%;
    }
    .signed .sign {
        justify-self: end;
        margin-top: clamp(6px, 1.2vw, 14px);
    }
    blockquote {
        max-width: 30ch;
        font-family: var(--serif);
        font-style: italic;
        font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.1rem + 2vw, 2.4rem);
        line-height: 1.22;
    }
    .sign {
        display: flex;
        align-items: flex-start;
        gap: 6px;
    }
    .cards {
        display: grid;
        gap: 32px 40px;
    }
    .card {
        display: grid;
        gap: 14px;
        align-content: start;
        padding-top: 18px;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
        color: var(--ink-2);
    }
    blockquote.small {
        max-width: none;
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        color: var(--ink);
    }
    cite {
        display: block;
        margin-top: 6px;
        font-family: var(--mono);
        font-size: 0.75rem;
        font-style: normal;
        color: var(--ink-3);
    }
    @media (min-width: 768px) {
        .cards {
            grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
        }
    }
</style>
